        notes: `# Chapter 4: Functions and Modular Programming\n\n## 4.1 The Need for Modularity\nImagine writing a program to manage a university's entire operations—student registration, course enrollment, fee payment, grading, and transcript generation. If you wrote all of this code in one giant block, it would be tens of thousands of lines long. It would be impossible to read, extremely difficult to find errors in, and if you needed to change the fee payment logic, you might accidentally break the grading logic.\n\n**Modular Programming** is the practice of breaking a large, complex program into smaller, independent, and manageable blocks of code. Each block handles one specific task. In programming, these blocks are called **Functions** (or methods, procedures, subroutines).\n\n## 4.2 What is a Function?\nA function is a self-contained block of code that takes some input, performs a specific operation, and optionally returns an output. Once a function is written, it can be reused (called) as many times as needed without rewriting the code.\n\n### Advantages of Using Functions:\n1.  **Reusability:** Write once, use everywhere (DRY principle: Don't Repeat Yourself).\n2.  **Readability:** Code is easier to understand when complex logic is abstracted into named functions like \'calculateTax()\$.\n3.  **Maintainability:** If a bug exists in the tax calculation, you only have to fix it in one place.\n4.  **Team Collaboration:** Different programmers can work on different functions simultaneously.\n\n## 4.3 Structure of a Function\nA standard function has four main parts:\n1.  **Return Type:** The data type of the result the function will send back (e.g., \'int\', \'float\', or \'void\' if it returns nothing).\n2.  **Function Name:** An identifier used to call the function. Should be a verb indicating action (e.g., \'printReceipt\').\n3.  **Parameters (Arguments):** Variables that act as placeholders for the data passed into the function.\n4.  **Function Body:** The actual code block enclosed in braces \'{}\'.\n\n### Pseudocode Example: A Simple Addition Function\n\'\'\'text\nFUNCTION calculateSum(number1, number2)\n    Total = number1 + number2\n    RETURN Total\nEND FUNCTION\n\n// Main Program\nResult = calculateSum(50, 30) // Result becomes 80\nPRINT Result\n\'\'\'\n\n## 4.4 Parameters vs. Arguments\n*   **Parameters:** The variables defined in the function declaration (e.g., \'number1\' and \'number2\').\n*   **Arguments:** The actual values passed into the function when it is called (e.g., \'50\' and \'30\').\n\n### Pass by Value vs. Pass by Reference\n*   **Pass by Value:** The function gets a *copy* of the variable. If the function changes it, the original variable in the main program remains unchanged.\n*   **Pass by Reference:** The function gets the *memory address* of the variable. Changes made inside the function permanently affect the original variable.\n\n## 4.5 Variable Scope\nScope refers to the accessibility of a variable in different parts of your code.\n*   **Local Variables:** Declared inside a function. They can only be seen and used inside that specific function. Once the function finishes running, the variable is destroyed.\n*   **Global Variables:** Declared outside all functions. They can be seen and modified by any function in the program. *Warning: Overusing global variables is bad practice because it makes bugs harder to track.*\n\n## 4.6 Built-in vs. User-Defined Functions\n*   **Built-in Functions:** Provided by the programming language (e.g., \'print()\', \'Math.sqrt()\', \'String.length()\'). You don't write the code for these; you just use them.\n*   **User-Defined Functions:** The custom functions you write yourself to solve specific problems.\n\n## 4.7 Nigerian Context: Modularizing a BVN Verification System\nLet's apply modular programming to a Nigerian fintech app (like OPay or PalmPay) attempting to verify a user's Bank Verification Number (BVN).\n\nInstead of one massive script, the system uses modular functions:\n\n1.  \'function formatBVN(bvnString)\' -> Cleans up the input, removing spaces.\n2.  \'function validateLength(bvnString)\' -> Returns True if it is exactly 11 digits.\n3.  \'function fetchBVNDetailsFromNIBSS(bvnString)\' -> Connects to the central server and retrieves the user's name and DOB.\n4.  \'function matchNames(bvnName, profileName)\' -> Checks if the name on the BVN matches the name on the app profile.\n\nThe main registration flow simply calls these functions in order. If the NIBSS API changes tomorrow, the programmer only needs to update \'fetchBVNDetailsFromNIBSS()\', leaving the rest of the app perfectly intact.\n\n## 4.8 Practice & Examination Questions\n\n### Section A: Conceptual\n1. Define modular programming and list three major advantages of using functions.\n2. What is the difference between a parameter and an argument?\n3. Differentiate between a local variable and a global variable. Why are global variables generally discouraged?\n\n### Section B: Algorithmic Translation\n4. Write the pseudocode for a function named \'calculateDiscount\' that takes two parameters: \'originalPrice\' and \'discountPercentage\'. The function should return the final price after the discount is applied.\n5. Write the pseudocode for a function named \'isEven\' that takes an integer as a parameter and returns TRUE if the number is even, and FALSE if it is odd.\n\n### Section C: Critical Thinking\n6. You are debugging a program where a variable's value is mysteriously changing, causing calculations to fail. How might variable scope (local vs. global) be the root cause of this bug? Explain your reasoning.`,
