        id: "csc299-t2",
        title: "Software development practices",
        status: "new",
        studyHistory: [],
        notes: `# Chapter 2: Software Development Practices\n\n## 2.1 The Software Development Lifecycle (SDLC)\nSoftware development is a systematic process, not just writing code. The **Software Development Lifecycle** provides a framework for building high-quality software reliably:\n\n### Traditional Waterfall Model\n- Linear, sequential phases: Requirements → Analysis → Design → Implementation → Testing → Deployment → Maintenance\n- Each phase must be completed before the next begins\n- Good for projects with clear, unchanging requirements\n- **Downside:** Inflexible to changes once development starts\n\n### Agile Methodology\n- Iterative and incremental approach\n- Work is organized into **sprints** (typically 2-4 weeks)\n- Emphasizes adaptive planning, evolutionary development, and early delivery\n- Key frameworks: Scrum, Kanban\n- **Nigerian Context:** Many tech startups in Lagos and Abuja use Agile for rapid product development\n\n## 2.2 Core Development Practices\n\n### Version Control (Git)\n- Tracks changes to source code over time\n- Enables collaboration among multiple developers\n- **Basic workflow:** Initialize repo → Create branch → Make commits → Pull Request/Merge\n- Popular platforms: GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket\n- **Why it matters:** Prevents code loss, enables code review, tracks history\n\n### Code Review\n- Peer examination of code before merging\n- Catches bugs, improves quality, shares knowledge\n- Follows style guidelines and project conventions\n- Common in professional teams and open-source projects\n\n### Testing\n- **Unit Testing:** Testing individual functions/components\n- **Integration Testing:** Testing how components work together\n- **Regression Testing:** Ensuring new changes don't break existing functionality\n- **Test-Driven Development (TDD):** Write tests before code\n- Popular frameworks: Jest, PyTest, NUnit\n\n### Documentation\n- **README:** Project overview, setup instructions\n- **Inline comments:** Explain why code does what it does\n- **API Documentation:** For public-facing interfaces\n- **User Manuals:** For end-users\n\n## 2.3 Team Collaboration\n\n### Roles in a Development Team\n| Role | Responsibilities |\n|------|-----------------|\n| Product Owner | Defines what to build, prioritizes features |\n| Scrum Master | Facilitates the process, removes obstacles |\n| Developer | Writes code, implements features |\n| QA Tester | Tests the software, finds bugs |\n| Designer | UI/UX design, user experience |\n\n### Communication Best Practices\n- Daily stand-up meetings (15 minutes)\n- Clear issue tracking\n- Regular demos/showcases\n- Honest progress reporting\n\n## 2.4 Nigerian Tech Ecosystem Context\n\nNigeria's tech industry has grown rapidly, with hubs in:\n- **Lagos:** "Silicon Valley of Africa," fintech, creative tech\n- **Abuja:** Government tech projects, startups\n- **Port Harcourt:** Oil & gas tech, emerging startups\n- **Kano:** AI, mobile technologies\n\nCommon practices in Nigerian tech companies:\n- Remote work is increasingly common\n- Many startups use international remote teams\n- Google Developer Groups (GDG), TechHubs support learning\n- Participation in tech competitions (Hackathons)\n\n## 2.5 Exam-Style Questions (For Revision)\n\n1. Explain the difference between Waterfall and Agile methodologies.\n2. What are the key practices of version control with Git?\n3. Why is code review important in professional software development?\n4. List and describe three types of testing every software project should have.\n5. How can a computer science student in Nigeria prepare for a career in software development?\n\n## 2.6 Summary\nProfessional software development involves disciplined processes, collaboration, and continuous quality improvement. Beyond writing code, successful developers version their work, test thoroughly, document their work, and collaborate effectively with their team. Understanding the SDLC and Agile methodologies is essential for anyone pursuing a career in software engineering.\n\n---
*Note: This content is adapted for Federal University, Gusau (FUGUS) curriculum and Nigerian context.*`,
