        id: "csc299-t3",
        title: "Hardware maintenance and troubleshooting",
        status: "new",
        studyHistory: [],
        notes: `# Chapter 4: Hardware Maintenance and Troubleshooting\n\n## 4.1 Introduction to Computer Hardware\nComputer systems consist of various physical components that work together to process, store, and output information. Understanding how to maintain and troubleshoot these components is essential for IT professionals.\n\n### Major Hardware Components\n| Component | Function |\n|-----------|----------|\n| **CPU (Central Processing Unit)** | The "brain" - executes instructions and processes data |\n| **RAM (Random Access Memory)** | Temporary working memory, volatile (lost when power off) |\n| **Storage (HDD/SSD)** | Permanent data storage, non-volatile |\n| **Motherboard** | Connects all components together, provides communication pathways |\n| **Power Supply Unit (PSU)** | Converts wall power to usable computer power |\n| **GPU (Graphics Processing Unit)** | Renders images and video for display |\n| **Input Devices** | Keyboard, mouse, webcam - user interaction |\n| **Output Devices** | Monitor, printer, speakers - user output |\n\n## 4.2 Basic Troubleshooting Methodology\nFollow this systematic approach whenever hardware issues arise:\n\n### Step 1: Identify the Symptoms\n- What exactly is happening? (beeping sounds, error messages, no power)\n- When does the problem occur? (on startup, during use, randomly)\n- Have there been recent changes (new hardware, updates)?\n\n### Step 2: Isolate the Problem\n- **Divide and conquer:** Test components one at a time\n- **Eliminate the obvious:** Check power cables, connections first\n- **Use symptoms as clues:** Beep codes indicate specific issues\n\n### Step 3: Test Components Individually\n- **Power supply test:** Verify PSU is providing correct voltages\n- **RAM test:** MemTest86 or built-in diagnostics\n- **Storage test:** Check SMART status, bad sectors\n- **Component substitution:** Replace known-good part if available\n\n### Step 4: Document and Fix\n- Record what was found and what fixed it\n- Apply the fix (replacement, repair, configuration)\n- Verify the issue is resolved before concluding\n\n## 4.3 Common Hardware Issues and Solutions\n\n### No Power (Computer Won't Turn On)\n- **Check:** Power cable connections, wall outlet, power switch\n- **Test:** Try different outlet or power cable\n- **Possible fixes:** Faulty PSU, motherboard issue, power button problem\n\n### Overheating\n- **Check:** Fan operation, dust buildup, ventilation\n- **Clean:** Remove dust from fans and heat sinks\n- **Replace:** Faulty thermal paste, failing fans\n- **Prevent:** Ensure proper airflow, use on hard surfaces\n\n### Blue Screen of Death (BSOD)\n- **Check:** Recent hardware changes, new installations\n- **Test:** MemTest86 for RAM, chkdsk for disk errors\n- **Possible fixes:** Reseat RAM, replace faulty hardware, update drivers\n\n### Peripheral Not Working\n- **Check:** Connections, power (for external devices), correct port\n- **Test:** Try different USB port or cable\n- **Update:** Drivers if needed\n- **Replace:** Faulty peripheral if others work\n\n## 4.4 Tools Every IT Student Should Have\n| Tool | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| Screwdriver set (Phillips/flathead) | Opening computer cases, component replacement |\n| Multimeter | Measuring voltage, current, continuity |\n| Anti-static wrist strap | Preventing electrostatic discharge damage to components |\n| Compressed air | Cleaning dust from fans and vents |\n| POST card / Diagnostic LED | Identifying POST errors |\n| Spare components (RAM, cable) | For testing and substitution |\n\n## 4.5 Nigerian Context: Hardware Challenges\n\nIn Nigeria, IT students and professionals face unique hardware challenges:\n- **Power instability:** Frequent power outages (NEPA/PHCN) damage components; use UPS (Uninterruptible Power Supply)\n- **Heat:** High ambient temperatures affect component lifespan; proper cooling is essential\n- **Import costs:** Replacement parts can be expensive due to import duties; handle components carefully\n- **Counterfeit parts:** Market sold "fake" components; buy from reputable suppliers\n- **Local repair culture:** Many Nigerian technicians develop strong troubleshooting skills through necessity\n\n## 4.6 Exam-Style Questions (For Revision)\n\n1. List the major hardware components of a computer and their functions.\n2. Describe the systematic troubleshooting methodology when a computer won't power on.\n3. What are the common causes of overheating and how can they be prevented?\n4. Why is an anti-static wrist strap important when working inside a computer?\n5. How does power instability in Nigeria affect computer hardware, and what solutions would you recommend?\n\n## 4.7 Summary\nHardware maintenance and troubleshooting are fundamental skills for IT professionals. By following systematic methodologies, using the right tools, and understanding common issues, you can diagnose and resolve most hardware problems. Always document your work and approach problems methodically rather than guessing.\n\n---
*Note: This content is adapted for Federal University, Gusau (FUGUS) curriculum and Nigerian context.*`,
